#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520473 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(1-3x)}^{8},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{8}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{8})^{′}=8{g}^{7}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =8{g}^{7}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-24(1-3x)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520484 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})(\\,4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520487 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{2x-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520516 :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+1}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\left(\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}\\right)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(0)+1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520663 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- x - 3 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,2 t^{2} - 4 t⟩\n$$ for $t\\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,2 t^{2} - 4 t⟩,\n$$ then along the curve we have $x=\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}$ and $y=2 t^{2} - 4 t.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{2} - 4 t)j \\\\ &=(8 \\sqrt{t})i+(4 t - 4)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(8 \\sqrt{t})^2+(4 t - 4)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{64t+16(t-1)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16(t^2+2t+1)} \\\\ &=4|t+1| \\\\ &=4 t + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t+1|=t+1$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- x - 3 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-1)\\left(\\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)+(-3)(2 t^{2} - 4 t) \\\\ &=- \\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} - 6 t^{2} + 12 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - x - 3 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(- \\frac{16 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} - 6 t^{2} + 12 t)\\cdot (4 t + 4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}- \\frac{64}{3}t^{3/2}(t+1)+(-48)(t^2/2-t)(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}- \\frac{64 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3} - \\frac{64 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} - 24 t^{3} + 24 t^{2} + 48 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\left(- \\frac{128 t^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{128 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{15}\\right)+(- 6 t^{4})+(8 t^{3})+(24 t^{2})\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(- \\frac{128 (2)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{128 (2)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{15}\\right)+(- 6 (2)^{4})+(8 (2)^{3})+(24 (2)^{2})\\right)-\\left(\\left(- \\frac{128 (0)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{128 (0)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{15}\\right)+(- 6 (0)^{4})+(8 (0)^{3})+(24 (0)^{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(- \\frac{1024 \\sqrt{2}}{21}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{512 \\sqrt{2}}{15}\\right)+(-96)+(64)+(96)\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)+(0)+(0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=64 - \\frac{8704 \\sqrt{2}}{105}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520666 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - 3$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [1,3].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(5)(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)i+(5)(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)j \\\\ &=(5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+(5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{25t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)} \\\\ &=5|t| \\\\ &=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - 3$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(2)\\left((5 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 5 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2\\right)+(-3) \\\\ &=(50)\\left(\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\right)+(-3) \\\\ &=(50)(1+t^2)+(-3) \\\\ &=50 t^{2} + 47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - 3\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}(50 t^{2} + 47)\\cdot (5 t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}250 t^{3} + 235 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\left(\\frac{235 t^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{125 t^{4}}{2}\\right)\\right]_{1}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(\\frac{235 (3)^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{125 (3)^{4}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{235 (1)^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{125 (1)^{4}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(\\frac{2115}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{10125}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{235}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{125}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=5940.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520670 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x + 2 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨- 2 t,\\,- 2 t - 2⟩$ for $t\\in [-2,0].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨- 2 t,\\,- 2 t - 2⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=- 2 t$ and $y=- 2 t - 2.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t - 2)j \\\\ &=(-2)i+(-2)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{8} \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2 x + 2 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(2)\\left(- 2 t+(- 2 t - 2)\\right) \\\\ &=- 8 t - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x + 2 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{0}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{0}(- 8 t - 4)\\cdot (2 \\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(2 \\sqrt{2})\\int_{-2}^{0}- 8 t - 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{0}- 16 \\sqrt{2} t - 8 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(4 \\sqrt{2})\\left[- 2 t^{2}+(- 2 t)\\right]_{-2}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left[(- 8 \\sqrt{2} t^{2})+(- 8 \\sqrt{2} t)\\right]_{-2}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left((- 8 \\sqrt{2} (0)^{2})+(- 8 \\sqrt{2} (0))\\right)-\\left((- 8 \\sqrt{2} (-2)^{2})+(- 8 \\sqrt{2} (-2))\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((0)+(0)\\right)-\\left((- 32 \\sqrt{2})+(16 \\sqrt{2})\\right) \\\\ &=16 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520686 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=9 - 2 x^{2}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [- \\frac{\\pi}{2},- \\frac{\\pi}{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})i+(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9\\sin^2t+9\\cos^2t} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{9} \\\\ &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=9 - 2 x^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-1)\\left(2(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2-9\\right) \\\\ &=(-9)(2\\cos^2t-1) \\\\ &=- 9 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 9 - 2 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}(- 9 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\cdot (3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}- 27 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-27\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\cos 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(-27)\\left[\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\ &=\\left[\\left(- \\frac{27 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(- \\frac{27 \\sin{\\left(2 \\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(- \\frac{27 \\sin{\\left(2 \\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(\\frac{27}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left((0)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520688 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x - 2 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨- t,\\,1 - t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨- t,\\,1 - t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=- t$ and $y=1 - t.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1 - t)j \\\\ &=(-1)i+(-1)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-1)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- 2 x - 2 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-2)\\left(- t+(1 - t)\\right) \\\\ &=4 t - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - 2 x - 2 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(4 t - 2)\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(\\sqrt{2})\\int_{0}^{3}4 t - 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}4 \\sqrt{2} t - 2 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(- 2 \\sqrt{2})\\left[- t^{2}+(t)\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left[(2 \\sqrt{2} t^{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2} t)\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\left((2 \\sqrt{2} (3)^{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2} (3))\\right)-\\left((2 \\sqrt{2} (0)^{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2} (0))\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((18 \\sqrt{2})+(- 6 \\sqrt{2})\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=12 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520693 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2} - 2$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(4)(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)i+(4)(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)j \\\\ &=(4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)} \\\\ &=4|t| \\\\ &=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2} - 2$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-2)\\left((4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 4 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(-32)\\left(\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(-32)(1+t^2)+(-2) \\\\ &=- 32 t^{2} - 34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2} - 2\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(- 32 t^{2} - 34)\\cdot (4 t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}- 128 t^{3} - 136 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(- 68 t^{2})+(- 32 t^{4})\\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\\\ &=\\left((- 68 (\\pi)^{2})+(- 32 (\\pi)^{4})\\right)-\\left((- 68 (0)^{2})+(- 32 (0)^{4})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((- 68 \\pi^{2})+(- 32 \\pi^{4})\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=\\pi^{2} \\left(- 32 \\pi^{2} - 68\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520721 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + 2 y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t⟩\n$$ for $t\\in [1,2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3},\\,\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t⟩,\n$$ then along the curve we have $x=\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t)j \\\\ &=(10 \\sqrt{t})i+(5 t - 5)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(10 \\sqrt{t})^2+(5 t - 5)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{100t+25(t-1)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{25(t^2+2t+1)} \\\\ &=5|t+1| \\\\ &=5 t + 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t+1|=t+1$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x + 2 y$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(1)\\left(\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right)+(2)(\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 5 t) \\\\ &=\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + 5 t^{2} - 10 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + 2 y\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}(\\frac{20 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + 5 t^{2} - 10 t)\\cdot (5 t + 5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{100}{3}t^{3/2}(t+1)+(50)(t^2/2-t)(t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{100 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3} + \\frac{100 t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + 25 t^{3} - 25 t^{2} - 50 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\left(\\frac{200 t^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{40 t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{25 t^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{25 t^{3}}{3}\\right)+(- 25 t^{2})\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(\\frac{200 (2)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{40 (2)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{25 (2)^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{25 (2)^{3}}{3}\\right)+(- 25 (2)^{2})\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{200 (1)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{21}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{40 (1)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{25 (1)^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{25 (1)^{3}}{3}\\right)+(- 25 (1)^{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(\\frac{1600 \\sqrt{2}}{21}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{160 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)+(100)+\\left(- \\frac{200}{3}\\right)+(-100)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{200}{21}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{40}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{25}{3}\\right)+(-25)\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{5245}{84} + \\frac{2720 \\sqrt{2}}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520724 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- y^{2}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [- \\frac{\\pi}{4},0].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})i+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16\\sin^2t+16\\cos^2t} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16} \\\\ &=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- y^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-1)(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2 \\\\ &=-16\\sin^2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - y^{2}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}(- 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}- 64 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-64\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}\\sin^2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-64\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-32\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}1-\\cos 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(-32)\\left[t-\\frac{\\sin 2t}{2}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left[(- 32 t)+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left((- 32 (0))+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 (0) \\right)})\\right)-\\left((- 32 \\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right))+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 \\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((0)+(0)\\right)-\\left((8 \\pi)+(-16)\\right) \\\\ &=16 - 8 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520725 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- \\frac{3}{- x + y}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨3 t,\\,3 t + 6⟩$ for $t\\in [-2,2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨3 t,\\,3 t + 6⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=3 t$ and $y=3 t + 6.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t + 6)j \\\\ &=(3)i+(3)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(3)^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{18} \\\\ &=3 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- \\frac{3}{- x + y}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=\\frac{-3}{(3 t + 6)-(3 t)} \\\\ &=\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{2}\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (3 \\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(3 \\sqrt{2})\\int_{-2}^{2}- \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\left[t\\right]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left[\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} t}{2}\\right)\\right]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} (2)}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} (-2)}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((- 3 \\sqrt{2})\\right)-\\left((3 \\sqrt{2})\\right) \\\\ &=- 6 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520728 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2} - 2$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(2)(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)i+(2)(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)j \\\\ &=(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{4t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)} \\\\ &=2|t| \\\\ &=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2} - 2$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(1)\\left((2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(4)\\left(\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(4)(1+t^2)+(-2) \\\\ &=4 t^{2} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x^{2} + y^{2} - 2\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(4 t^{2} + 2)\\cdot (2 t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}8 t^{3} + 4 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(2 t^{2})+(2 t^{4})\\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\\\ &=\\left((2 (\\pi)^{2})+(2 (\\pi)^{4})\\right)-\\left((2 (0)^{2})+(2 (0)^{4})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((2 \\pi^{2})+(2 \\pi^{4})\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\pi^{2} \\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520740 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y},\\,4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4 x^{2} y + 2 e^{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x y^{2} + 2 e^{x}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=8 x y,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=8 x y.\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}s &=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(16 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x^{2} y\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(24 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\left[12 y^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521464 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-1\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t)+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{({t}^{2}-t)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521469 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ $y=\\pm 2.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨y,\\hspace{0.222em}x,\\hspace{0.222em}2⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{2}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521475 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=2z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+x\\,j+3y\\,k \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-14{sin}^{2}⁡t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-7(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-5+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-5t+\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-5(2\\pi )+0)-(1-5(0)+0) \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521681 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 x + y^{2})\\,i+(2 x y + 8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-2) & =(12 (-1) + (-2)^{2})\\,i+(2 (-1) (-2) + 8 (-2))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(-12)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,-2)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-(-2)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨x + 1,\\,y + 2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521684 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 10 x + 5 y^{2})\\,i+(10 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}) & =(- 10 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) + 5 \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(10 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) + 4 \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{185}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{55}{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521695 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x - 5 y^{2})\\,i+(- 10 x y + 12 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3}) & =(4 \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) - 5 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(- 10 \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) + 12 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{85}{9}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{13}{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521716 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{2} - 5 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(4 x - 5 y^{2})\\,i+(- 10 x y + 12 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,3) & =(4 (-1) - 5 (3)^{2})\\,i+(- 10 (-1) (3) + 12 (3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-49)\\,i+(66)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-49,66⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-(3)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-49,66⟩\\cdot ⟨x + 1,\\,y - 3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521719 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6 x^{2} - 6 x y^{2} - 5 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 x - 6 y^{2})\\,i+(- 12 x y - 10 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2}) & =(12 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) - 6 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(- 12 \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right) - 10 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{111}{2}\\right)\\,i+(-20)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{111}{2},-20⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521722 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6 x^{2} + x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(12 x + y^{2})\\,i+(2 x y + 8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =(12 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(2 \\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + 8 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{119}{4}\\right)\\,i+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{5}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{119}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521733 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 5 x^{2} - 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 5 x^{2} - 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 5 x^{2} - 4 x y^{2} + 4 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 10 x - 4 y^{2})\\,i+(- 8 x y + 8 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,3) & =(- 10 (-2) - 4 (3)^{2})\\,i+(- 8 (-2) (3) + 8 (3))\\,j \\\\  & =(-16)\\,i+(72)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-16,72⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-(-2),\\,y-(3)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-16,72⟩\\cdot ⟨x + 2,\\,y - 3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521734 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x + 6 y^{2})\\,i+(12 x y + 12 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =(6 \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) + 6 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2})\\,i+(12 \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + 12 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,i+(3)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right),\\,y-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨- \\frac{3}{2},3⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{1}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521735 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3 x^{2} + 6 x y^{2} + 6 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 x + 6 y^{2})\\,i+(12 x y + 12 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =(6 (3) + 6 (1)^{2})\\,i+(12 (3) (1) + 12 (1))\\,j \\\\  & =(24)\\,i+(48)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨24,48⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,1)\\cdot ⟨x-(3),\\,y-(1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨24,48⟩\\cdot ⟨x - 3,\\,y - 1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521743 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 10 x + 5 y^{2})\\,i+(10 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,3) & =(- 10 (3) + 5 (3)^{2})\\,i+(10 (3) (3) + 4 (3))\\,j \\\\  & =(15)\\,i+(102)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨15,102⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-(3),\\,y-(3)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨15,102⟩\\cdot ⟨x - 3,\\,y - 3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521756 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $8 x y + 4 y^{2}=- 4 x^{2} + 2 y + 2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 2 y + 2\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 2&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 2$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 2)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 2)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 2)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-2(-x)-(2)&=0\\\\2x-(2)&=0\\\\2x&=2\\\\x&=1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(1,-1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521758 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $8 x y + 2 y^{2}=16 x^{2} + 432$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 2 y^{2}&=16 x^{2} + 432\\\\- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 432&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 432$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 432)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 432)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 4 y)\\,i-(- 32 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\32 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=4x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=4x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2(4x)^{2}+8x(4x)-16x^{2}-432&=0\\\\48x^{2}-432&=0\\\\x^{2}&=9\\\\x&=\\pm 3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(3,12)$ and $(-3,-12)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521761 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $4 x y + y^{2}=4 x^{2} + 8$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4 x y + y^{2}&=4 x^{2} + 8\\\\- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 8&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 8$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 8)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + y^{2} - 8)\\,j\\\\&=(4 x + 2 y)\\,i-(- 8 x + 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\8 x - 4 y&=0\\\\y&=2x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}1(2x)^{2}+4x(2x)-4x^{2}-8&=0\\\\8x^{2}-8&=0\\\\x^{2}&=1\\\\x&=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521800 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $8 x y + 3 y^{2}=16 x^{2} + 1024$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 3 y^{2}&=16 x^{2} + 1024\\\\- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 3 y^{2} - 1024&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 3 y^{2} - 1024$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 3 y^{2} - 1024)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 3 y^{2} - 1024)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 6 y)\\,i-(- 32 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\32 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=4x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=4x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}3(4x)^{2}+8x(4x)-16x^{2}-1024&=0\\\\64x^{2}-1024&=0\\\\x^{2}&=16\\\\x&=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(4,16)$ and $(-4,-16)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521807 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $8 x y + 2 y^{2}=16 x^{2} + 48$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 2 y^{2}&=16 x^{2} + 48\\\\- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 4 y)\\,i-(- 32 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\32 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=4x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=4x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2(4x)^{2}+8x(4x)-16x^{2}-48&=0\\\\48x^{2}-48&=0\\\\x^{2}&=1\\\\x&=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(1,4)$ and $(-1,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521812 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $8 x y + 4 y^{2}=- 4 x^{2} + 3 y - 12$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 3 y - 12\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 3)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-3(-x)-(-12)&=0\\\\3x-(-12)&=0\\\\3x&=-12\\\\x&=-4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-4,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521815 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $8 x y + 4 y^{2}=- 4 x^{2} + 2 y + 12$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 2 y + 12\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 2)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-2(-x)-(12)&=0\\\\2x-(12)&=0\\\\2x&=12\\\\x&=6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(6,-6)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521832 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $8 x y + 4 y^{2}=- 4 x^{2} + 4 y - 12$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 4 y - 12\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 4)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-4(-x)-(-12)&=0\\\\4x-(-12)&=0\\\\4x&=-12\\\\x&=-3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-3,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521863 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $2 x y + y^{2}=- x^{2} + 3 y - 15$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2 x y + y^{2}&=- x^{2} + 3 y - 15\\\\x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15)\\,j\\\\&=(2 x + 2 y - 3)\\,i-(2 x + 2 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 2 x - 2 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}1(x+(-x))^{2}-3(-x)-(-15)&=0\\\\3x-(-15)&=0\\\\3x&=-15\\\\x&=-5\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-5,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521870 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $4 x y + 2 y^{2}=4 x^{2} + 48$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4 x y + 2 y^{2}&=4 x^{2} + 48\\\\- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,j\\\\&=(4 x + 4 y)\\,i-(- 8 x + 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\8 x - 4 y&=0\\\\y&=2x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2(2x)^{2}+4x(2x)-4x^{2}-48&=0\\\\12x^{2}-48&=0\\\\x^{2}&=4\\\\x&=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521880 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{25{t}^{2}+7},\\,5t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+7},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k\n$$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{2k}{5}\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+7},\\,0\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521893 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨4t,\\,6\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 5 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨20u,\\,6\\sqrt{5u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=5k\n$$ is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{9x},\\,0\\le x\\le 20.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ only."}]}
